
Following is from the 1/87 issue of the Atlanta FIG newsletter, 
The Forth Estate:

DON SCHRADER demonstrated his Novix based development board 
running in an IBM AT. The system ran 10 million empty loops in 3 
seconds and recompiles itself in about 4 seconds. Don says that 
Harris should have a product out by April. Also Software Composers 
has changed their name to Silicon Composers. The High Tech 
Revolution BBS is up and running at 951-9251 and caters to Forth 
and AI interests.

BRIAN WALSH brought in his HP-28C which may be the ultimate 
scientific calculator. Its Forth heritage is very obvious with 
keys for DUP, SWAP, OVER, etc. The calculator has a 4 line, 23 
character display, symbolic math processing, and plotting 
capabilities.

NICK HENNENFENT brought along the Computone 9620, an NEC V20 
based, battery powered laptop. The machine has an 8x80 display and 
a built in thermal printer. Software for the machine is all 
written in Forth including policy illustration programs for the 
insurance industry, a communications program, a spreadsheet, and a 
word processor. The machine is completely menu driven, has a built 
in print spooler, and provides a hot key to toggle between any two 
applications.


